  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 233 PM EDT Thu Sep 10 2026\n\nScattered to isolated showers remain mostly to our north, closer to \na weak frontal boundary. However, some of our northern most areas \nmay still see an isolated shower or storm this afternoon and \nevening. The frontal boundary was previously forecast to push into \nour area tonight and Friday. However, it now looks like the front \nmay stay to our north. This means Friday will now be drier with only \nisolated showers, and perhaps a few storms, in place during the \nafternoon and evening hours. \n\nPrecip chances do increase Friday night into Saturday as a weak \nshortwave moves across the region. The best coverage of showers and \nstorms will be on Saturday. A few strong storms and wind gusts are \npossible, but the main threat will continue to be locally heavy \nrainfall due to elevated PW values up around 2 inches. While \nisolated flooding potential exists, it's low. Clouds and \nshowers/storms may prevent CHA from reaching 90 again on Saturday, \nbut if they do hit 90 the consecutive streak with likely continue \ninto next week.\n\nPrecip chances lessen on Sunday as drier air filters in from the \nnorthwest behind the shortwave, though a few isolated showers and \nstorms remain possible.\n\nHigh pressure then looks to build back into the area to start the \nweek. This means the heat will remain with no meaningful rain \nchances. Though the east TN mountains may still see some isolated \ndiurnal activity. By mid-to-late week, a broad upper trough and \nanother frontal boundary approach from the north. As a result, precip \nchances increase during this timeframe due to more zonal flow and \nhopefully a frontal passage.",
